WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.750
- [Narrator] In early 2019,

00:00:01.750 --> 00:00:04.980
Robert Pattinson was announced
as the new cape crusader

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.030
for Matt Reeves' highly
anticipated adaptation,

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:09.410
The Batman.

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.030
And well, the internet,

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:12.120
they had thoughts.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:14.100
(shouting)
- Noooooo!

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:15.960
- [Narrator] Which is pretty
much par for the course

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:18.280
whenever someone is cast in a Batman movie

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.010
or any superhero movie.

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:21.210
- It's like, "Ben
Affleck's gonna be Batman."

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.238
The first reviewer goes,

00:00:22.238 --> 00:00:24.870
"Noooo!"
(laughs)

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:26.470
- [Narrator] And being
as Robert Pattinson's

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.787
most recognizable role to date was this...

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:32.900
- This is the skin of a killer, Bill.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.730
- [Narrator] We can understand
the initial skepticism.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.740
More recently, though,

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:39.870
that skepticism has shifted
to cautious optimism

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.810
as people have discovered
the non-Twilight parts

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.370
of Pattinson's career.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.420
As the movie heads back into production,

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.740
we thought we'd ask,

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:51.180
how did Robert Pattinson go
from Twilight to the Batman

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.100
and why do we and a lot of initial haters

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:56.120
now think he's the perfect choice?

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.760
To do this, we really
have to take a look back.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:00.760
Before we examine how Robert Pattinson

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:01.950
emerged as the front runner

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.060
in the most coveted
superhero role, like ever,

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:06.410
we have to examine

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.750
what makes a good
superhero on the big screen

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:11.320
and how that's evolved over the years.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.500
Christopher Reeves
really kicked things off

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.530
with Superman in the late 70s.

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.540
Yeah, we're really going back that far.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:20.250
But our idea of a modern day superhero

00:01:20.250 --> 00:01:23.010
really doesn't take effect until the 2000s

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:25.400
when superhero movies dominated

00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.330
the mainstream blockbuster season.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:30.810
Prior to the 2000s, studios
looking to cast a superhero

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.380
or a superhero villain,

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:34.730
tended to go straight
to big name movie stars

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.560
and just put them in shiny suits.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.370
Sometimes, those suits had nipples.

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:40.510
Studios thought,

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:42.250
if you're gonna be able
to sell these movies

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.940
to McDonald's and MasterCard,
you gotta have big names.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:47.220
And, they were right.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.140
For the most part,

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.000
big names equal big
dollars at the box office.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.120
But when they assembled the
team for the 2000's X-Men,

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.830
the previous formula for what
made a superhero completely,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:01:59.810
well, mutated.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:02.430
By implying an inventive
mix of award winners

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:04.840
and prestigious actors with newcomers and

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:07.100
not really including any marquee names,

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.400
X-Men set itself apart from
all other superhero movies

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:12.470
and it was a huge success.

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.610
Okay, Halle Berry was a big time star but

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:18.230
she also pretty much
told us why she was cast.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.420
And, a couple of years later, Sam Raimi,

00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.170
would take that new casting
formula even further

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:24.100
with Spider-Man,

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.130
picking critical darling, Tobey Maguire,

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.360
no marquee name at the
time, to play Peter Parker.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:32.120
After 2000, it helped
more to have an Oscar nom

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.210
than $100 million movie behind you,

00:02:34.210 --> 00:02:36.810
if you wanted a studio
to put you in spandex.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.210
This is not to say Hollywood

00:02:38.210 --> 00:02:40.760
abandon the movie star
formula all together,

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.740
but as we get into the mid 2000s,

00:02:42.740 --> 00:02:46.040
that strategy was yielding
diminishing returns.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:48.960
And right as the superhero
genre is really taking off

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.380
and studios are rethinking

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.420
who to cast as their world savers,

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:54.710
Robert Pattinson, starts acting.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.550
Cast as Reese Witherspoon's
son in Vanity Fair,

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.230
yes, the same Reese who
would play his love interest

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:02.930
just seven years later,
which is not weird at all,

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.010
Patterson's role for Vanity
Fair was unceremoniously cut

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:07.120
at the last minute.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:10.210
The casting director for that
film reportedly felt so bad

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.090
that they cast him as the
uber-charming, Cedric Diggory,

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:15.514
in Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire.

00:03:15.514 --> 00:03:17.940
- So, I was quite glad
I got cut in the end.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.850
- [Narrator] After bouncing around

00:03:18.850 --> 00:03:20.290
some forgettable indies,

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.590
in 2007, Robert Pattinson
audition for the role

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.990
of Edward Cullen in Twilight.

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.460
Never expecting it to be a blockbuster hit

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.030
and thinking it would just
be another forgettable indie,

00:03:31.030 --> 00:03:33.360
Pattinson was then inadvertently thrust

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.690
into one of the biggest franchises

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:39.240
to ever hit tween girls'
walls and the pages of Tumblr.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.548
- [Announcer] Get loud for Edward Cullen

00:03:40.548 --> 00:03:42.370
(mumbling)
(cheering)

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:45.270
- [Narrator] Then, Twilight
began breaking records in Hall H

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.740
and at the box office, making Pattinson

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:49.370
the exact kind of movie star

00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:52.120
that would have been perfect
for a superhero movie,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.600
once upon a time.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.850
While our paths turned
out Twilight sequels

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.690
and subtly shaded the film
and the fandom in interviews

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.900
every chance he got...

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.630
- Did your Twilight experience

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.683
turn out to be what you expected?

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:06.780
(deep exhaling)

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.240
- [Narrator] Over at superhero land,

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.660
something interesting is taking flight.

00:04:10.660 --> 00:04:13.760
Marvel and Christopher Nolan,
leaned into the X-Men model

00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:16.100
by hiring actors-actors and unknowns

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:19.300
and both start dominating
at the box office.

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.440
Although these are household names now,

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.780
looking back on the faces

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.700
of superhero movies from this time,

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.590
it was more of a who's who of, who's that?

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.460
Back then, Robert Downey Jr. was

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.380
mostly known for his arrest record,

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.020
Chris Hemsworth was an Aussie

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:35.460
with a barely there Star Trek cameo,

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:38.140
Jennifer Lawrence had been
nominated for an Oscar but

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.110
for a film that was out
grossed by Piranha 3D

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:43.710
and Henry Cavill, before Man of Steel,

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:46.520
he was best known for
playing the only character

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:50.070
to have more bed partners than
Henry VIII, on the Tudors.

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:52.260
But, back to our new Batman.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:55.370
When Pattinson left The
Twilight Zone in 2012,

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:59.360
after four films and $3.3
billion at the box office,

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:01.450
he could essentially
do anything he wanted.

00:05:01.450 --> 00:05:02.680
And what he wanted to do,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:04.900
was work with some of
the most innovative indie

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:07.110
and avant garde directors out there.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.100
He worked with names like Warner Hertzog,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:12.870
David Cronenberg, David Michelle,
Claire Denis, James gray,

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:15.400
the Safdie brothers and Robert Eggers,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:18.300
avoiding anything big
budget or high profile.

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:20.790
By, not auditioning, 'cause he's bad at it

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:23.320
and essentially hounding
the directors he admired,

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:27.050
Pattinson amassed an impressive
filmography of varied roles,

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:28.690
as well as critical acclaim

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.810
and a reputation of someone with taste

00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.360
that many creative directors sought out.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.290
This was also the moment
that studios started hiring

00:05:36.290 --> 00:05:39.000
auteur directors to film superhero films.

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:42.070
This was not to say that every
effort was the Dark Knight

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:45.320
but, in this timeframe,
the most successful efforts

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:49.080
for fans and critics, shied
away from big movie stars.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.430
And, yes, we are marking Justice League

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:55.470
as a lackluster effort, but we
are reserving final judgment

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:57.070
until we see the Snyder Cut.

00:05:57.070 --> 00:06:00.580
If all this auteur stuff is
sounding somewhat familiar,

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:03.300
let us remind you of
these award winning actors

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:06.412
who also eventually chose to
play comic book characters,

00:06:06.412 --> 00:06:08.100
(growling)

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:08.933
or at least I think

00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:11.378
that's what Tom Hardy was doing in Venom.

00:06:11.378 --> 00:06:12.916
I'm still not quite sure.

00:06:12.916 --> 00:06:15.300
- Like a turd, in the wind.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:16.480
- [Narrator] So this brings us around

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.920
to the time we found out Robert Pattinson

00:06:18.920 --> 00:06:20.840
would be our next Batman.

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.440
From what we know about the Batman,

00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:23.530
weird auteurism

00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:25.640
and a bit of weirdness
in the cape and cowl

00:06:25.640 --> 00:06:28.040
is exactly what the film needs.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:31.220
This is not gonna be an
early 2000 superhero flick.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.800
Rumored to follow the plot
of The Long Halloween,

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.860
this new Batman is more
of a mob detective movie.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.400
Don't look for too many all out brawls,

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:41.980
which is probably a good
thing since Pattinson said

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.268
that he doesn't plan to
work out for the role.

00:06:44.268 --> 00:06:45.890
(sighs)
Perish the thought!

00:06:45.890 --> 00:06:48.540
With that in mind,
Pattinson's unique filmography

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.620
actually gives us a ton of evidence

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.780
as to why he was perfectly cast.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.620
In the certified fresh High Life

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:57.850
as an abandoned astronaut
floating aimlessly in space,

00:06:57.850 --> 00:07:00.420
Pattinson and showed his
ability to be captivating

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:02.010
in a psychological thriller

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:03.570
as the brooding young billionaire

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.300
riding around New York for
Cronenberg's Cosmopolis,

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:09.050
he gives us a tiny peek
into what he could bring

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.770
to a young Bruce Wayne.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:14.350
And, as the affluent pioneer
bent on winning his lady love,

00:07:14.350 --> 00:07:15.990
no matter the cost for Damsel,

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:18.390
he shows us how he can
portray Bruce Wayne's

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:22.170
fanatical quest to seek
justice, by any means necessary.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.370
It's hard to know what
made Matt Reeves think

00:07:24.370 --> 00:07:26.590
Pattinson would be perfect for the role,

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:28.370
well, besides a killer jawline,

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.160
but you can see how
Pattinson gained an edge by

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:33.650
simply being as weird as possible.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.263
And, I mean, he was really weird.

00:07:36.263 --> 00:07:39.190
(mumbling)
(thumping)

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:41.330
But again, being uber-weird

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:44.220
is something men who successfully
don the cape and cowl

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:47.780
kinda have in common.
(screaming)

00:07:47.780 --> 00:07:48.830
I mean, we get it,

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.930
if you haven't been
following his filmography

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.890
and you heard the sparkle
vamp was gonna be Bruce Wayne,

00:07:53.890 --> 00:07:55.400
you're likely...

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:58.650
- [Woman] Stop!
(clinks)

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.970
- [Narrator] But, very few
people knew that Heath Ledger

00:08:00.970 --> 00:08:03.920
would be able to bring the
Cobb gravitas to the Joker,

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.530
that Michael Keaton could pull off

00:08:05.530 --> 00:08:08.090
the aloof and mysterious but ultra swab,

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:09.990
sometimes love struck billionaire,

00:08:09.990 --> 00:08:12.180
or that Hugh Jackman
could give a character

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:14.680
that he'd been playing
for almost two decades,

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:16.320
the heartbreaking swan song

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:19.246
of a warrior that's seen
way too many battles.

00:08:19.246 --> 00:08:20.850
(sighs)

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:21.853
Still makes me cry.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.590
We might have guessed that Ryan Reynolds

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:26.980
could have quipped and joked his way

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:28.390
to a pitch perfect Deadpool,

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:30.070
but we doubt even he thought

00:08:30.070 --> 00:08:31.760
it would be as awesome as it was.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:34.550
Chris Evans himself was
doubtful in his ability

00:08:34.550 --> 00:08:36.580
to pull off the perpetually patriotic

00:08:36.580 --> 00:08:38.600
and wholesome, Captain America.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:40.970
And, thank God they
convinced him otherwise,

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.930
but does anyone remember the
fact that it took Ryan Reynolds

00:08:43.930 --> 00:08:47.811
three films to get a quippy
superhero performance to work.

00:08:47.811 --> 00:08:48.644
I'm just saying.

00:08:48.644 --> 00:08:51.560
More than anything, what makes
Pattinson perfect for Batman

00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:52.860
is what he's not.

00:08:52.860 --> 00:08:56.500
Clooney, Kilmer and Affleck
brought plenty of star power

00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.010
but they left fans and critics cold.

00:08:59.010 --> 00:09:01.340
From the drunken mermaid
loving light keeper,

00:09:01.340 --> 00:09:04.390
to the charismatic bank robber,
to the criminal astronaut,

00:09:04.390 --> 00:09:06.400
to the hilariously offensive monarch,

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:09.860
to the post apocalyptic
survivor who hums pop ballads,

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:12.250
Pattinson has proved his dogged commitment

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.700
to finding interesting characters
no matter where they lie.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.370
And all that work solidified

00:09:17.370 --> 00:09:19.400
to make casting Pattinson as Batman

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.660
a compelling and inspired choice,

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.260
right around the time that Warner Brothers

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:26.610
was looking for a new young
actor to play Bruce Wayne.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:29.380
Knowing all this, it kinda
makes sense to cast a man

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.040
who went on the Today Show
and lied about dead clowns.

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:34.770
- First time I went to
a circus somebody die,

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:37.030
one of the clowns died.
(laughs)

00:09:37.030 --> 00:09:40.580
- [Narrator] And he totally
lied about those dead clowns.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.840
Honestly, in the end,

00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:45.100
the only thing I'm looking
forward to more than the film

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:47.304
is Pattinson on the Batman press tour.

00:09:47.304 --> 00:09:48.137
- In the sex shop?

00:09:48.137 --> 00:09:49.210
Batman is not a superhero.

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.490
I got a very pronounced gag reflex.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:53.520
My rap's name was Big Tub.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:54.353
I just always thought like,

00:09:54.353 --> 00:09:55.840
have you ever see this
movie, Corky Romano?

00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:56.680
I used to be friends with

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:58.564
all the hookers who lived down the street.

00:09:58.564 --> 00:10:00.510
(laughs)

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.163
Sometimes.
(chuckles)

00:10:02.163 --> 00:10:03.050
Sometimes!

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:05.180
- [Narrator] Let your
freak flag fly my boy.

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:06.013
Let it fly.

